-- title:
--   Construct the first graph-sketch layer in native array storage
-- statement:
--   Fix a graph on $n$ vertices, a dimension $k$, and a trial bit string $\omega$ of the prescribed length. Its decoded signs give labels $\sigma$ and sketch signs $R$. Suppose the native headers, sign database and unary templates contain $n,k$, the trial length, and the counts $n,k,nk$, with the stated lookup and wedge scratch stacks empty. The first-layer program prepends to the previous-layer database the framed arrays
--   $$
--   \bigl(\operatorname{denseLayer}(1,v,a)\bigr)_{v<n,\,a<k}
--   $$
--   in vertex-major, state-minor order. All other state components and the previous database tail are preserved. Writing $W=100(1+n+k)^2$, its cost is at most
--   $$
--   (C_{\rm vertex}+31)n+1000(W+1)(n+1)+2000(W+1)2^k(nk+1),
--   $$
--   where $C_{\rm vertex}$ is the verified budget for loading one vertex label and computing its $k$ initial-state arrays. This initializes the exterior graph dynamic program.
-- source:
--   T. Koana, Approximate Counting of k-Paths in O*(2^k) Time, STOC 2027 submission #953, October 2026, §3.2 (printed pp. 8–9 / PDF pp. 9–10) and §4.1 (printed p. 9 / PDF p. 10), https://github.com/sigact-stoc/stoc2027/releases/download/papers-2/953.pdf. Native implementation: Solutions/FirstLayer.lean, line 22; source snapshot SHA-256 8d71f1dc6550c0ad0be64cfc6619103f55137589e62480502f6379e729795c0f.

